<BaseLayout title="About" description="Learn about Shreya Roongta’s path from teaching and creating educational resources to graduate study in Learning Design and Technology."><header class="page-hero"><div class="shell narrow"><p class="eyebrow">About</p><h1>From teaching to designing for learning</h1><p class="lede">My work is grounded in the questions educators ask every day: Who are my learners, what do they need, and how can I help them make meaning?</p></div></header><section class="section shell about-grid"><aside><p class="pull-quote">“Teaching showed me that the way a learning experience is designed can shape how someone participates, understands, and grows.”</p></aside><div class="prose"><h2>My story</h2><p>I began my career as a teacher in India and later moved to the United States. Across my experience in teaching, early childhood education, and working directly with learners, I have seen how much thoughtful planning matters. A well-designed lesson or resource can make an idea clearer, invite curiosity, and give a learner a meaningful way into the work.</p><p>Developing educational resources drew me beyond the delivery of a lesson and toward the decisions behind it. I became increasingly interested in how people learn, how curriculum creates a path through complex ideas, and how technology can strengthen an experience when it is used with care.</p><section class="journey-panel" aria-labelledby="tpt-journey"><p class="eyebrow">Teacher-created resources</p><h2 id="tpt-journey">My TPT journey</h2><p>My classroom experience taught me how much educators value resources that are thoughtful, engaging, and ready to use. I created <strong>Artful Learning Printables</strong> to carry that work into a digital space and share learning materials with a wider community of teachers and families.</p><p>The store brings together art, academics, and imagination through printable and digital resources for young learners. Creating materials across literacy, math, visual arts, and seasonal learning has given me another way to think about clear instructions, purposeful practice, visual design, and the needs of both learners and educators.</p><a class="button tpt-link" href="https://www.teacherspayteachers.com/store/artful-learning-printables">Visit Artful Learning Printables <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section><h2>Why learning design</h2><p>Learning design brings those interests together. It gives me a way to build on my experience as an educator while thinking more deeply about audience, objectives, content, interaction, and reflection. I chose graduate study in Learning Design and Technology to develop this practice with greater intention.</p><p>As I continue my studies, I am exploring curriculum development, instructional design, and educational technology. My direction is toward learning design, instructional design, or curriculum-development work in education or EdTech—work that keeps learners at the center and turns thoughtful ideas into useful experiences.</p><a class="button" href={withBase('projects/')}>See how the work develops</a></div></section></BaseLayout>
